# Compile semantic notebooks with measured flow and fixed regions

## Scope and existing evidence

Paste text or Markdown, preserve headings, paragraphs and lists as notebook
blocks, and paginate them onto the selected physical sheets. Expose overflow
and fixed-region refusals instead of silently clipping content.

This is unfinished implementation work. The following original design
records remain accepted theoretical deliverables and are not reopened:

- ATR-P2-001 — archived semantic design
- ATR-P2-003 — archived semantic design
- ATR-P2-004 — archived semantic design
- ATR-P2-005 — archived semantic design

## Native CLI plain-text and Markdown flow (partial) — 2026-11-03

The Rust `atrament-profiles notebook` CLI now accepts an explicitly supplied
`--flow-text` or `--flow-markdown` source, or `--flow-text-file` and
`--flow-markdown-file` for caller-selected bounded UTF-8 files (maximum
4,096 bytes). It uses the selected saved paper profile's validated physical
printable rectangle, exact Unicode grapheme boundaries and the same nominal
per-glyph horizontal advances as the native authored-stroke compositor.

When a handwriting statistics profile is selected, the mean width/spacing
and height/baseline affect flow; without one, advances are 4 mm and the
minimum row pitch is 9 mm. Page breaks are automatic, up to 32 pages and
128 laid-out lines; the preexisting 512 total pen-segment export cap remains.
An explicit `--pages` smaller than the measured PDF count refuses.

The limited Markdown subset recognizes `#`, `##`, and `###` single-line
headings and `-` or `*` list items. Headings default to an explicit ink
underline, while `--heading-style marker` can place a colored four-stroke
opaque highlight **behind** authored heading ink; `none` suppresses the
heading mark without changing text. List items keep their distinct physical
ink mark and indentation. Heading styling is CLI-only and does not create
semantic block styles or persistent title identities.

Plain-text lines wrap independently at word or grapheme boundaries;
Markdown ordinary lines now combine as soft-wrapped paragraphs, with two
trailing spaces preserving a hard row break. Blank lines add paragraph
spacing; Markdown headings and lists end pending body paragraphs before
their own styled rows. One-to-nine-digit `1.`/`1)` numbered items now retain
their literal label as exact authored glyph ink, indent continuations and
do not receive synthetic bullet strokes. Missing digit or separator shapes
refuse at native export. Long headings refuse their one-line fixed region.
This does **not** implement inline Markdown styling, nested lists,
semantic list identities or custom marker letterforms.

Only caller-authored exact glyph strokes render; missing visible shapes still
refuse with codepoints instead of silently substituting font characters.

Native regression tests rendered real two-page PDF and separate 72-DPI PNG
pages from a heading, paragraph, list and subsequent body lines. Additional
tests cover decomposed Unicode glyph wrapping without dividing a grapheme,
selected paper margins, exact file-vs-inline output, invalid UTF-8/oversize
file refusal, and immutable existing output on oversized headings, missing
shapes and insufficient explicitly requested pages.

Four further native regressions distinguish Markdown soft-wrapped Unicode
paragraphs from exact plain-text and explicit two-space hard breaks. They
verify preserved decomposed graphemes, heading/list boundaries, original
ink expansion, blank-line spacing, and one versus two physical pages for the
same 40 source lines. Existing two-page PDF/PNG and seeded variation tests
now use deliberate hard breaks instead of relying on non-Markdown line
semantics. Real PDF/PNG parity and atomic empty-heading/list refusal are
also verified. This is still a restricted export-only parser.

Three additional ordered-list tests verify mixed heading, decimal and
parenthesized numbering alongside bullet marks; native PDF and transparent
PNG use the same user-authored numerals and delimiters. Wrapped numbered
items retain their indentation without duplicating the label, and a
10-digit marker remains ordinary text instead of silently accepting an
unsupported list syntax. Missing number glyphs and empty numbered items
refuse before replacing an existing PDF. Numbering is not computed or
persisted in a semantic document.

The source and resulting ink are export-only CLI state, **not** a
persisted semantic notebook model.
Browser paste/edit, stable semantic block identities, complete Markdown,
fixed image regions, true glyph outlines, editable aesthetic title styles
and persisted notebook body content remain open.

This partial slice does not meet the finite two-page saved-notebook
acceptance criterion.

## Acceptance decision

Open. The finite implementation described above is not established by
the original theoretical design acceptance. This follow-up must be
evaluated separately against its practical or theoretical scope.
